{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Hannah, welcome to Brightline! I checked your account. It's active and not locked, and it's set up to ask you for a new password the first time you sign in, so your temporary password is working. The error most likely comes from the new password you're choosing. Please try this: 1) Sign in on your laptop's Windows sign-in screen, not a web page, while you're in the office or connected to VPN. 2) When it asks for a new password, use at least 14 characters. A short phrase works well, like three or four words with a number. 3) Check that Caps Lock is off and the keyboard language is right. Look out for characters that are easy to mix up, like l and I, or 0 and O. If it still fails, please reply and tell us where you're signing in (the laptop screen or a web page) and the exact wording of the error. A screenshot or photo is great. Then we can take the next step.",
  "internal_note": "KB-003 checks done. lookup_user (id 12): status active, must_change_password=true, failed_login_count=0, not blocked, password_last_set 2026-10-09 (the temporary password), device BL-LT-0112. Sign-in logs: no failed attempts and nothing unusual; all sign-ins are from the corporate network or a home network in New York during work hours. Account is not locked, so no unlock is needed. The temp password is accepted, so no reset is needed yet. The most likely cause is the new password failing the 14-character / no-reuse policy, or the change being tried on a web page instead of the Windows sign-in screen on the office network or VPN. Sent the user the KB-003 fix steps and asked where she's signing in and the exact error. Next steps: if it still fails at the Windows sign-in screen with a password that meets the policy, consider issuing a new temp password per KB-002. If the account shows active with no change required and no password works, escalate per KB-003.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-003"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
